    "baseRefName": "main",
    "body": "## Summary\n\n- distinguish minimal factory learning from the harder target of open-ended coherent modification;\n- use Naur to state a scoped functional requirement: some project-specific state must perform the mapping, justification, and integration work of program theory;\n- keep the carrier open \u2014 theory may be implicit or distributed, while natural-language theory is the specific addressable realization under test;\n- stop treating search, trajectories, learned policies, and optimization as automatically substituting for theory, since they may construct, revise, apply, or embody it;\n- restore partial and fallible theory, tentative change, backtracking, recovery, and theory revision as the realistic model of software development;\n- preserve the experimental boundary: a natural-language-theory intervention tests that explicit surface, and a factory-learning claim additionally requires changed reusable family machinery to affect later production.\n\n## Files\n\n- the research-program article;\n- the neutral factory-learning mechanism comparison;\n- the note on theory-mediated heterogeneous factory development.\n\nThe article title is deliberately unchanged in this PR.",
